 "lines": [
  {
   "id": "i01a",
   "text": "F. Fighter.",
   "item": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "i01b",
   "text": "It's the most famous letter in military aviation, and today it covers multirole jets that also strike targets on the ground.",
   "item": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "i01c",
   "text": "The F-35 should have been the F-24, the next number in line. Instead, it kept the number of its demonstrator, the X-35.",
   "item": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "i01d",
   "text": "And the F-117 Nighthawk got an F, even though it was built for ground attack.",
   "item": 0
  },
  {
   "id": "i02a",
   "text": "A. Attack.",
   "item": 1
  },
  {
   "id": "i02b",
   "text": "An attack aircraft is built to hit targets on land or at sea.",
   "item": 1
  },
  {
   "id": "i02c",
   "text": "Today, the A-10 is the only aircraft in the Air Force that still carries a plain A.",
   "item": 1
  },
  {
   "id": "i03a",
   "text": "B. Bomber.",
   "item": 2
  },
  {
   "id": "i03b",
   "text": "Bombers fly long-range strike missions.",
   "item": 2
  },
  {
   "id": "i03c",
   "text": "The newest one is the B-21. Its number was chosen because it's the first bomber of the twenty-first century. By the rules, it would have been the B-3.",
   "item": 2
  },
  {
   "id": "i04a",
   "text": "C. Cargo.",
   "item": 3
  },
  {
   "id": "i04b",
   "text": "C means transport, like the C-130 Hercules.",
   "item": 3
  },
  {
   "id": "i04c",
   "text": "And the C-130 shows how the whole system works. Add one more letter in front, and the same airframe becomes a gunship, a rescue plane, a special operations plane, an electronic warfare plane, or a tanker.",
   "item": 3
  },
  {
   "id": "i05a",
   "text": "K. Tanker.",
   "item": 4
  },
  {
   "id": "i05b",
   "text": "Put a K in front of a C, and you get a tanker, like the KC-46.",
   "item": 4
  },
  {
   "id": "i05c",
   "text": "K exists as a basic mission too, but no ordinary aircraft has ever been given it the way the rules intended.",
   "item": 4
  },
  {
   "id": "i05d",
   "text": "Some names simply copy the maker. A tanker sold to other countries became the KC-767, straight from Boeing's model number.",
   "item": 4
  },
  {
   "id": "i06a",
   "text": "E. Electronic.",
   "item": 5
  },
  {
   "id": "i06b",
   "text": "As a modified mission, E means electronic warfare, like the EC-130.",
   "item": 5
  },
  {
   "id": "i06c",
   "text": "As a basic mission, it means special electronics, for aircraft built around radars and sensors.",
   "item": 5
  },
  {
   "id": "i07a",
   "text": "H. Rescue, and helicopter.",
   "item": 6
  },
  {
   "id": "i07b",
   "text": "H can show up twice in the same name.",
   "item": 6
  },
  {
   "id": "i07c",
   "text": "In the HH-60G, the first H means search and rescue, and the second H means helicopter. Many of them started life as Black Hawks that were modified for rescue.",
   "item": 6
  },
  {
   "id": "i08a",
   "text": "M. Multi-mission.",
   "item": 7
  },
  {
   "id": "i08b",
   "text": "M aircraft fly combat and special operations, like the MC-130.",
   "item": 7
  },
  {
   "id": "i08c",
   "text": "And the MQ-9 Reaper reads as multi-mission, unmanned, design number nine.",
   "item": 7
  },
  {
   "id": "i09a",
   "text": "U. Utility.",
   "item": 8
  },
  {
   "id": "i09b",
   "text": "U aircraft fly support missions, like the UH-60 Black Hawk.",
   "item": 8
  },
  {
   "id": "i09c",
   "text": "Utility was once used as a cover. The U-2 spy plane was labeled utility to hide what it really did.",
   "item": 8
  },
  {
   "id": "i10a",
   "text": "R. Reconnaissance.",
   "item": 9
  },
  {
   "id": "i10b",
   "text": "R is for spy missions.",
   "item": 9
  },
  {
   "id": "i10c",
   "text": "The SR-71 Blackbird was set to be called the RS-71, but Air Force chief Curtis LeMay preferred SR, for strategic reconnaissance. And SR is what stuck.",
   "item": 9
  },
  {
   "id": "i11a",
   "text": "W. Weather.",
   "item": 10
  },
  {
   "id": "i11b",
   "text": "W means weather reconnaissance.",
   "item": 10
  },
  {
   "id": "i11c",
   "text": "That's why the Air Force's hurricane hunters are WC-130s.",
   "item": 10
  },
  {
   "id": "i12a",
   "text": "V. VIP, and vertical takeoff.",
   "item": 11
  },
  {
   "id": "i12b",
   "text": "In front, V means VIP transport. The President's 747 is the VC-25, and his newest helicopter is the VH-92.",
   "item": 11
  },
  {
   "id": "i12c",
   "text": "Before the dash, V means vertical or short takeoff, like the MV-22 Osprey.",
   "item": 11
  },
  {
   "id": "i13a",
   "text": "L. Cold weather.",
   "item": 12
  },
  {
   "id": "i13b",
   "text": "L means modified for cold weather. The LC-130 has skis, for landing on ice.",
   "item": 12
  },
  {
   "id": "i14a",
   "text": "Q. Unmanned.",
   "item": 13
  },
  {
   "id": "i14b",
   "text": "Q means drone.",
   "item": 13
  },
  {
   "id": "i14c",
   "text": "There's even a letter for the ground stations that fly them: D, for the U A V control segment.",
   "item": 13
  },
  {
   "id": "i15a",
   "text": "P. Patrol.",
   "item": 14
  },
  {
   "id": "i15b",
   "text": "P means maritime patrol, for aircraft that watch the sea.",
   "item": 14
  },
  {
   "id": "i15c",
   "text": "Today's main one is the P-8 Poseidon, a Navy jet built on a Boeing 737.",
   "item": 14
  },
  {
   "id": "i16a",
   "text": "S. Anti-submarine.",
   "item": 15
  },
  {
   "id": "i16b",
   "text": "S is for hunting submarines. Before the dash, S means something completely different: a spaceplane.",
   "item": 15
  },
  {
   "id": "i16c",
   "text": "The S-3 Viking flew from aircraft carriers to hunt submarines.",
   "item": 15
  },
  {
   "id": "i17a",
   "text": "T. Trainer.",
   "item": 16
  },
  {
   "id": "i17b",
   "text": "The T-6 Texan Two skipped the numbers four and five, as a tribute to the original T-6 trainer.",
   "item": 16
  },
  {
   "id": "i18a",
   "text": "O. Observation.",
   "item": 17
  },
  {
   "id": "i18b",
   "text": "O means observation, like spotting targets for other aircraft. The new OA-1K Skyraider Two borrowed the name of the old A-1 Skyraider.",
   "item": 17
  },
  {
   "id": "i19a",
   "text": "X. Experimental.",
   "item": 18
  },
  {
   "id": "i19b",
   "text": "In front of everything, X means experimental. That's where the F-35 started, as the X-35.",
   "item": 18
  },
  {
   "id": "i19c",
   "text": "Experimental aircraft flown by manufacturers or by NASA often get X numbers too.",
   "item": 18
  },
  {
   "id": "i20a",
   "text": "Y. Prototype.",
   "item": 19
  },
  {
   "id": "i20b",
   "text": "The jet that won the competition was the YF-22. The one that entered service was the F-22.",
   "item": 19
  },
  {
   "id": "i21a",
   "text": "G. Grounded.",
   "item": 20
  },
  {
   "id": "i21b",
   "text": "As a prefix, G means permanently grounded. Before the dash, G means glider.",
   "item": 20
  },
  {
   "id": "i22a",
   "text": "J and N. Special test.",
   "item": 21
  },
  {
   "id": "i22b",
   "text": "J means a temporary special test, and the aircraft goes back to normal service. N means it never does.",
   "item": 21
  },
  {
   "id": "ya",
   "text": "The whole system dates to nineteen sixty-two. Before that, the Navy and the Air Force named their aircraft differently, and the same fighter was the F4H Phantom to one and the F-110 to the other.",
   "item": -1
  },
  {
   "id": "yb",
   "text": "After the change, it became simply the F-4.",
   "item": -1
  },
  {
   "id": "za",
   "text": "And after the letters come the numbers. In nineteen sixty-two, the counts were restarted, which is why they suddenly got small again.",
   "item": -1
  },
  {
   "id": "zb",
   "text": "The last letter marks the version: A, then B, then C. I and O are skipped, so they're never confused with one and zero.",
   "item": -1
  },
  {
   "id": "zb2",
   "text": "And the number thirteen has been skipped in many series, for superstition.",
   "item": -1
  },
  {
   "id": "zc",
   "text": "Which aircraft name should I decode next? Tell me in the comments.",
   "item": -1
  }
 ]
}